{"tool_name":"pybedgraph","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"pybedgraph","identifiers":["doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btaa061"],"license":"MIT","summary":"A package for fast operations on 1-dimensional genomic signal tracks","home_url":"https://github.com/TheJacksonLaboratory/pyBedGraph","keywords":["Chromatin","Genome","Genomics","High-Throughput Nucleotide Sequencing"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":8},"biotools":{"id":"pybedgraph","home":"https://github.com/TheJacksonLaboratory/pyBedGraph","license":"MIT","summary":"A Python package for fast operations on 1-dimensional genomic signal tracks.\n\nFinds the mean, approx. mean, max, min, coverage, or standard deviation for a given interval in a bedGraph file.\n\nPartly written in Cython for speed improvements.","addition_date":"2021-01-18T07:33:28Z","last_update_date":"2021-01-30T14:33:31Z","biotools_curie":"biotools:pybedgraph","credit":[{"email":"minji.kim@jax.org","name":"Minji Kim","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7282-8224","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Formatting","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0335"},{"term":"Sorting","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3802"},{"term":"Standardisation and normalisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3435"}]}],"name":"pyBedGraph","owner":"zsmag19","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/BIOINFORMATICS/BTAA061","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2020 The Author(s). Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved.Motivation: Modern genomic research is driven by next-generation sequencing experiments such as ChIP-seq and ChIA-PET that generate coverage files for transcription factor binding, as well as DHS and ATAC-seq that yield coverage files for chromatin accessibility. Such files are in a bedGraph text format or a bigWig binary format. Obtaining summary statistics in a given region is a fundamental task in analyzing protein binding intensity or chromatin accessibility. However, the existing Python package for operating on coverage files is not optimized for speed. Results: We developed pyBedGraph, a Python package to quickly obtain summary statistics for a given interval in a bedGraph or a bigWig file. When tested on 12 ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, RNA-seq and ChIA-PET datasets, pyBedGraph is on average 260 times faster than the existing program pyBigWig. On average, pyBedGraph can look up the exact mean signal of 1 million regions in 0.26 s and can compute their approximate means in <0.12 s on a conventional laptop.","authors":[{"name":"Chuang J.H."},{"name":"Kim M."},{"name":"Ruan Y."},{"name":"Zhang H.B."}],"date":"2020-05-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"PyBedGraph: A python package for fast operations on 1D genomic signal tracks"},"pmcid":"PMC7214040","pmid":"32044918"}],"topic":[{"term":"ChIP-seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3169"},{"term":"Chromosome conformation capture","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3940"},{"term":"RNA-Seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3170"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"pyBedGraph","home":"https://bio.tools/pybedgraph","license":"MIT","summary":"A Python package for fast operations on 1-dimensional genomic signal tracks.\n\nFinds the mean, approx. mean, max, min, coverage, or standard deviation for a given interval in a bedGraph file.\n\nPartly written in Cython for speed improvements.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:pybedgraph/undefined/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":true,"interpreted":true,"license":true,"public":true,"readme":true,"repository":{"anonymous":true,"online":true}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"lic